          "body": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title>The Repairability Index — The Ledger Review</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://ledger-review.example/2026/07/the-repairability-index\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Four years of teardown data, and what it says about who is actually building repairable hardware.\">\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"NewsArticle\",\"headline\":\"The Repairability Index\",\"description\":\"Four years of teardown data, and what it says about who is actually building repairable hardware.\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Marguerite Okonjo\"},\"datePublished\":\"2026-07-14\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-07-15\",\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"The Ledger Review\"},\"isAccessibleForFree\":false,\"hasPart\":{\"@type\":\"WebPageElement\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":false,\"cssSelector\":\".paywalled-body\"}}</script>\n</head>\n<body>\n<article>\n<h1>The Repairability Index</h1>\n<p class=\"byline\">By Marguerite Okonjo · <time datetime=\"2026-07-14\">14 July 2026</time></p>\n<p class=\"standfirst\">Four years of teardown data, and what it says about who is actually building repairable hardware.</p>\n<p>We have taken apart 612 consumer devices since 2022. The pattern that survives every cut of the data is not the one the marketing departments would like.</p>\n<div class=\"paywalled-body\" data-paywall=\"subscriber\">\n<p class=\"paywall-notice\">This article is for subscribers. <a href=\"/subscribe\">Subscribe</a> to keep reading.</p>\n<p>Start with the fasteners. Of the devices we opened, the ones that came apart without heat, solvent or a spudger were overwhelmingly the ones built by companies that also sell spare parts, which is a duller finding than the one the press release promised and a more useful one.</p>\n<p>Adhesive is the variable that predicts everything else. Once a battery is glued rather than tabbed, the service manual stops being written, the parts catalogue stops being published, and the third-party repair market stops forming. None of those are consequences of the glue. They are consequences of the decision the glue records.</p>\n<p>The second pattern is quieter and harder to argue with: the firms whose scores improved did so in the year after a regulator published a scoring rule of its own, and not before. Voluntary disclosure moved nothing we can measure in four years of teardowns.</p>\n<p>What follows is the device-by-device table, the scoring rubric we used, and the three cases where we changed a score after the manufacturer wrote to us.</p>\n</div>\n</article>\n</body>\n</html>\n",
          "truncated": false
        }
      }
